(11. Next Steps) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The next experiment is to rerun Faster R-CNN with more stable training settings:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(* Lower learning rate: 1e-4 instead of 5e-4.) Tj
T*
(* Gradient clipping: max norm 1.0.) Tj
T*
(* More epochs with best-checkpoint selection.) Tj
T*
(* Separate output folders so older results are preserved.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(After that, the recommended next comparisons are:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(* Compare best-checkpoint Faster R-CNN against a detector such as RetinaNet.) Tj
T*
(* Try more pathology-specific stain augmentation or stain normalization.) Tj
T*
(* Analyze false positives by scanner to understand what visual patterns the detector) Tj
T*
(  confuses with mitotic figures.) Tj
T*
(* Consider adversarial/domain adaptation after establishing a stable detector baseline.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(12. Limitations) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(These results are based on the prepared MIDOG++ breast cancer subset and scanner holdout) Tj
T*
(splits used for the demo. They should not be interpreted as final challenge-test) Tj
T*
(performance. The hidden or official challenge test set was not used here. The current work) Tj
T*
(is best understood as a controlled project demo showing scanner-domain generalization issues) Tj
T*
(and possible mitigation strategies.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
